WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.030
- One stranger I still remember

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:04.200
that I actually will never forget

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.550
because he changed my life.

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:07.920
No matter how hard I try to forget

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.650
I'll never forget him actually.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:11.700
So it was a guy that I was helping out.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.300
I kind of sort of knew him, but
he was basically a stranger.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.290
But I didn't get any bad vibes off of him.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:18.430
He seemed like a good guy.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:19.363
Never hit on me.

00:00:20.756 --> 00:00:21.970
Never gave me a bad feeling at all,

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:23.910
but he needed help to get back home

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:27.880
to get to a job so he could
work to get back to his kids.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.250
So I'm a sucker story for...

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:32.210
Or a sucker for a kid story
and I wanted to help him out.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.380
So I said, "Well, you know,
I'm not working right now.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.000
I have the time. I have the
money. I have the vehicle.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.060
Have family in Texas. How
about I just drive you?"

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.553
And so I decided to drive him.

00:00:40.553 --> 00:00:43.163
Decided to drive the trip to Texas.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.690
He was driving when we got to Billings

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:48.410
and all of a sudden he changed.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.590
I mean the look in his eyes was different.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:55.400
He looked evil. He didn't
look like him anymore.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.820
And he started to hit me
in the head and shoulders

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:02.910
and he was trying to bash my
head off the dash of the car

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.170
but I was actually in really
good shape at the time

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.790
'cause I was the lead installer
for Great Energy Solutions.

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.990
So I had real big upper
body. I worked in attics.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.720
So I was able to stop myself
from him hitting my head,

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:13.553
and I'm laughing there

00:01:13.553 --> 00:01:15.830
because I was ornery and fiery

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.580
and I wasn't gonna let him do

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:18.780
whatever he was gonna do easily.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.610
So every step of the way,

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.740
for the two and a half days
that he had me, I fought him.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.150
You know, I paid for everything that I did

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.040
but at the same time,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.050
every time I pissed him off or hurt him

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:29.883
or just one of these things,

00:01:29.883 --> 00:01:31.430
it was a victory for me, it kept me going.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.200
I'd given up like the second night.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.100
I'd given up pretty much.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.710
I was curled up in the
passenger seat in kind of a ball

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:38.470
'cause he'd been hitting me
again in the back of the head

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.470
and on the shoulders and
on the back and everything.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.190
So I just kind of curled
up crying, given up,

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:44.090
just waiting for it to end.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:45.890
He said that he was gonna
take me up to the mountains,

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:47.970
kick the out of me, and
kick me off the mountain.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:49.950
And so I'm thinking, "Okay,
I'm gonna die now. Great."

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.620
But I'm thinking, "I'm
gonna take him with me.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.230
He's not gonna be able to
do this to anybody else.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.280
He's gonna pay for what he's done to me."

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.680
So I was like, "Okay,
fine. This is gonna happen.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.440
Hopefully, I can try to do
something when I get there."

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.640
But then I started thinking.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.740
My dad's gonna have to identify my a body.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:08.700
And that's actually what
stopped me from a accepting it

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.610
and trying to think of
how to get out of it.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.150
And see now I'm gonna end up crying.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.895
But I'm not. It's okay.

00:02:13.895 --> 00:02:15.330
Because I got out of it.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.190
But yeah, I couldn't fathom the fact

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:18.800
that my dad was gonna
have to identify my body.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.050
'Cause if he's gonna kick
the out of me, you know,

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.350
what am I gonna look like?

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.230
So I started thinking,
"What am I gonna do?

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.060
What do they do in Lifetime
network movies, right?"

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.220
So I Lifetime networked
him, is what I say.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.940
So I told him that I loved him,

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:34.667
I forgave him for everything he did to me,

00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:35.950
and I wouldn't abandon him.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.240
I think this is the key to him.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:38.400
I told him I wouldn't abandon him

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.590
no matter how bad he'd hurt me.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.820
At this point, we had
gotten off the highway

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:43.850
and we're on a gravel road.

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:45.450
But when I said that to him,

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.290
he started crying quietly

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:49.690
and he actually slowed down
the car and stopped it.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.540
He said something like, "Look,
what you made me do," maybe.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.700
Turned around, got back on the highway.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:55.700
And then the next morning,

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.460
he let me out in Fort Collins, Colorado.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:00.500
He gave me my car back at that same time.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:03.450
And I was free. I got my life back.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.250
Two and a half days.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.530
So yeah, I'll never forget him.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.740
I would have to say that I'm
a lot more leery of people.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.730
But I told myself I wasn't
gonna let it affect me

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:13.620
on how I help people.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:14.590
I like to help people.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:16.040
I think people deserve a chance.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:17.750
I think people deserve a second chance.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.840
I'm always gonna be friends.
I'm always gonna be positive.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.830
I just try not to be so naive. (chuckles)

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.370
I'm not gonna let anybody
get in my car, you know?

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.840
Even if I've known him
for a short period of time

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:27.920
and they seem like a good person,

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:29.300
no, we're not gonna do that.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:30.520
Don't let anybody get in your car.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:33.100
The more you tell a story by
the way, the less power it has.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.600
So if you ever have anything
traumatic happened to you,

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:36.433
talk about it.

00:03:36.433 --> 00:03:38.460
You gotta talk about it.
Don't hold it inside.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.390
- A stranger I still remember

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:43.380
is someone I met on the bus, actually.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.350
He was asking his friend
what stop was coming up.

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.990
He was trying to find his stop.

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:51.380
And I overheard and responded.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.510
I was like, "Oh no, it's
actually the next stop."

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:54.450
And then he asked me. He stopped.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:55.840
He's like, "Do you speak Spanish?"

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.230
And I was like, "No, actually I don't.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.440
I just speak English and
a little bit of French."

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:00.680
He's like, "That's super weird.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:03.460
I just asked that question in Spanish."

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:04.970
And I still think about it to this day

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:08.360
because I have no idea how I
understood what he was saying,

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.420
but I totally helped him find his stop.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.810
And yeah, I still think
about it. (chuckles)

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:13.960
- [Thoraya] How did you respond to him,

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:15.120
I'm curious, when he said?

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.360
- Yeah, he was just so
shocked and I was shocked,

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.701
and all my friends and
his friends were shocked

00:04:19.701 --> 00:04:21.260
and we're just like, "What is happening?"

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:22.840
Because he barely spoke English.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:25.285
I very much do not speak Spanish.

00:04:25.285 --> 00:04:29.070
Yeah, it was super crazy. (chuckles)

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:31.650
- I remember this old lady.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:34.040
So I used to work at Trader Joe's in LA

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:36.350
and she came through my
line, she was really sweet.

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:37.817
And I remember her saying like,

00:04:37.817 --> 00:04:40.930
"Oh, you know I'm gonna have
a little charcuterie board

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:41.763
by myself."

00:04:41.763 --> 00:04:43.979
And I was just like,
"Oh, where do you live?

00:04:43.979 --> 00:04:46.010
Like, I'll go hang out with you."

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.090
And she just started kind of giggling.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:51.650
And I remember she got like all blushy

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.180
and was just like, "Oh no.
No one wants to hang out

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.013
with an old lady."

00:04:55.013 --> 00:04:58.220
And so we had like a really
pleasant conversation

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:00.429
and then she left.

00:05:00.429 --> 00:05:01.670
And then like a few minutes later,

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.770
she comes with a piece of...

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:05.230
Actually, no, it wasn't
even piece of paper.

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:06.170
She just told me straight up.

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:08.820
She's like, "Oh, hey, by
the way, I live in..."

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:10.790
And then she gives me her address.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:14.180
And she's like, "If you wanna
come anytime before six,

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.470
you know, I would very
much appreciate it."

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:18.357
And I just remember being like

00:05:18.357 --> 00:05:20.020
"Oh my God, like, yes, please."

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:22.080
But I didn't have a pen and paper

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:23.676
so I didn't remember the address,

00:05:23.676 --> 00:05:25.910
and then I never saw her again.

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:28.360
But I just remember feeling so terrible

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:31.350
'cause I imagine she was
probably waiting for me

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:32.660
and I never showed up.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.440
So that's one stranger I remember.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.430
And I think I'll remember her forever,

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:39.830
and I'll always think about her.

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:42.380
- I don't know his name. I
don't think I ever got his name.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:45.050
But I was walking through Singleton Park,

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:49.470
which is this big, beautiful
park in Swansea in Whales,

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:50.370
South of Wales.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.740
And it was at a time of
day like this, like dusk,

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:57.150
and the colors were, it's
kind of pastel pink and blue,

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:58.700
just like it is right now.

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:02.750
And this guy, he's just in this tweed coat

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:04.850
and he's riding his bike and
he had to look on his face

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:07.520
that was just so content and happy

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.670
to be riding his bike through
a park in his tweed coat.

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:13.442
And then I saw him in the library

00:06:13.442 --> 00:06:14.275
and he was a librarian.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:18.530
And I had a brief interaction with him.

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:21.030
Asked him where a reference
book was, he told him,

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:22.270
and it was just very in keeping

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:25.120
with his kind of pleasant,
content demeanor.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:26.790
And yeah, that just stayed with me.

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:29.880
That sense of contentment
that he seemed to just exude.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:31.840
I think that some

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:36.070
people just have like inherent,

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:37.330
I don't know, goodness of them.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:39.610
There's like this quality
that comes through

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.680
in their affectation
and their interactions

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:46.230
that some person, a stranger

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:47.600
that you can meet in five minutes

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.440
and know that you could trust
leaving your kids with them

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:51.450
kind of person.

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:53.173
And he seemed to be of that ilk.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:57.310
- I was in New York city
and I think I was moving in,

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.580
and I had like a big box
of all kinds of stuff.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:01.250
I went to like Office Depot or something.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:02.850
And I was carrying this box

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:04.600
and it was like summer and it was hot.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:05.880
And this man on the street,

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:09.530
he told me I was too pretty
to be carrying that box.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:12.910
And I was like, "He's so right." (laughs)

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:14.520
Like when I'm carrying a box,

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:15.726
I just think about him

00:07:15.726 --> 00:07:19.720
and how he wouldn't want me
to be doing that. (laughs)

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:22.600
- One stranger I still remember is,

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:23.910
I was having a terrible day

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:25.860
and I was walking down the
street and really angry,

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:27.060
but I was wearing a really cute outfit.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:29.700
I felt really good, but I was sad.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:31.300
And this man on the corner of the street

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:34.730
yelled out at me, "Like a
young Katherine Hepburn."

00:07:34.730 --> 00:07:36.710
And I like think about it often.

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:39.560
And when I'm getting ready
in the morning and stuff

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:41.300
I'm like, "Oh, would this get someone

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.330
to like yell out at me?"

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:45.367
And now when I'm shopping for clothes,

00:07:45.367 --> 00:07:48.640
"I'm like would Catherine
Hepburn wear this?

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:50.297
Is this within the aesthetic?"

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:52.650
Yeah, it was very influential,

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:54.970
but in the best way possible. (laughs)

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:57.810
- [Thoraya] Have you ever
wanted to get into acting?

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:58.850
- I mean, yeah.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:00.430
You know, maybe that's another reason why

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:03.650
it was a really exciting acknowledgement

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:05.080
because I used to act a lot,

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:07.990
and I stopped when I moved to the city

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.650
and really focused on being a student.

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:13.780
And it's something that
I secretly love a lot

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:16.200
and I like to go to plays
and I sing in the shower,

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:19.730
but it's something that I haven't
been doing in a long time.

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:22.280
And so maybe that's another layer.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:26.520
Maybe we're getting at
some psychological level

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.940
to the stranger's impact on me. (laughs)

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:31.430
- One stranger I still remember
is on my fitness journey.

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:33.560
I have lost almost 60 pounds,

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:34.490
and I went to the gym

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:37.010
and I just took my shirt off
to look at myself one day.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:39.490
Random dude, never met him,
never talked to him before.

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:41.680
He just said, "Hey, man,
you look really good."

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:43.010
Me and my friends that I work out with,

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:44.220
we struggle a lot with our body.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:45.400
It's body dysmorphia.

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:46.310
A lot of people know about that,

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:48.990
and like you're never really
satisfied with yourself.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:50.680
You're never gonna be
happy with the way you look

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:51.810
and I'm still not.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:54.070
But like just that one
person you never talk to,

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:55.800
they just tell you that,

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:57.770
it goes a long way 'cause it
makes you feel a lot better

00:08:57.770 --> 00:08:59.850
about yourself and who you are.

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:01.900
- It was the first time I
felt like pretty in my life.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:04.117
This guy came up to me and said,

00:09:04.117 --> 00:09:06.510
"You have a very like
curious look to your face."

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:08.540
And I had no idea what that meant

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:09.910
but I thought that was a really cool way

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:11.380
to like compliment someone.

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:13.280
It was the first time
I've even complimented

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.500
other than like physical...

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:18.700
Like your general physical looks.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.100
'Cause I've always wanted to be someone

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:21.300
who asked a lot of questions

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:23.670
and sees the world in a different way

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:25.560
and I thought that curious
was a good way to say that.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:27.140
'Cause I am a very curious person

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:29.190
and I was cool that I
portrayed that to someone else

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:31.070
and someone picked up on that.

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.300
- There was this one time
I was at a gymnastics meet

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.060
and I was like nine years old.

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:37.600
I was walking by this stranger

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:39.080
and I said to him, I said, "Oh, I'm sorry.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.460
I need to pass through. I'm sorry."

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:42.830
And he looked at me. He said,
"What are you sorry for?"

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:44.720
And I was like, "Oh,
well, I'm in your way.

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:46.560
Like, I'm sorry for interrupting you."

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:48.780
And he was like, "You
didn't do anything wrong.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:49.900
Don't apologize."

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:53.620
I was just taken aback probably
because I was so young.

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:55.210
And I talked to my dad about it after

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:57.070
I was like, "That guy was
being a little bit rude,"

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:58.950
and he was like, "He was just teaching you

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:00.260
a good life lesson."

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:02.510
And it's true, like he really just was.

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:04.070
I think it's just very important

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:06.340
to use your words intentionally.

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:09.930
And if we keep saying
sorry for everything we do,

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:12.940
then we lose the value
of being apologetic.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:15.690
And so I just really value

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:17.830
when people people apologize
to me and mean it now.

00:10:17.830 --> 00:10:19.700
And I do the same vice versa.

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:22.453
- Okay. This was in Quebec city.

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:27.143
And there was some tourists,
beautiful day like today.

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:32.550
And a lovely family, a husband, wife and

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:35.900
young child from China,

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:38.710
this is like their
first trip out of China.

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:40.397
And so he came up to me.

00:10:40.397 --> 00:10:42.960
"Can I take a photo?" he said.

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:45.300
And I said, "Certainly.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.760
I'll take a photo of you and your wife."

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.627
He said, "No, no, no. I want
a photo of you and my wife."

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:58.570
So I posed and he took a
photo of me and his wife.

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:00.840
And then I offered to take a photo

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.610
of him and his family and "No way."

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:07.200
So it was like I was a curiosity to him.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.070
- So I don't know his name.
I don't know who he was.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:11.343
When I was a kid, I was
like walking our dogs.

00:11:11.343 --> 00:11:13.150
I remember I saw this dude.

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:16.920
He was bald, like totally
normal-looking San Diego guy.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:18.800
And he was walking his dog

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:20.097
and I remember thinking,

00:11:20.097 --> 00:11:22.340
"Isn't it so funny how
you see all these people

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:24.490
in your life that you don't remember

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:25.600
and then you forget them?"

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:28.430
And I have remembered that dude forever

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:29.713
because of that thought.

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:33.880
Told you it wasn't a good
story. It's such a lame story.

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:35.700
But literally I think
about that all the time.

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:37.750
Like just how you see all these people.

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:38.583
You never remember them,

00:11:38.583 --> 00:11:39.950
but I remember this one, bald dude

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:43.240
walking his German shepherd
by the park where I grew up.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:44.720
And that's my stranger. (chuckles)

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:46.880
- There's this cartoon
character named Uncle Ruckus

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:48.160
on "The Boondocks".

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:51.130
And I was at the Euclid
Trolley Station down here

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:53.150
and I seen this person with a little eye

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:55.960
and he looked just like
him with the braids.

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:57.660
That's where I knew characters is real,

00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:02.660
'cause like I just knew the
sense of the story is real life.

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:04.720
You know what I mean? It was crazy.

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:06.300
- [Thoraya] You think
it was him in real life.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:07.315
- It had to be him.

00:12:07.315 --> 00:12:08.280
(Thoraya laughs)

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.370
Hey, nah, 'cause he was mean too.

00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:11.971
He was yelling. He was yelling.

00:12:11.971 --> 00:12:14.737
I was just thinking like,
"Wow, that's crazy."

00:12:14.737 --> 00:12:15.620
You know what I mean?

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:20.120
- So I remember the
person but not his name.

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:20.953
- [Thoraya] Okay.

00:12:20.953 --> 00:12:22.670
- I met him

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:25.300
hiking on the John Muir Trail

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:28.953
in the High Sierras last September.

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:34.457
I remember him because

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:41.303
he was very open with his heart,

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:46.520
sharing his experience of
this magical place that

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:49.813
had touched my heart.

00:12:53.115 --> 00:12:54.282
It's like the,

00:12:57.160 --> 00:12:59.010
the most beautiful thing in the world

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:03.170
to see something that
fills your heart but then

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:13.040
you've got no one to share it with.

00:13:14.307 --> 00:13:15.557
And he shared it with me.

00:13:17.416 --> 00:13:20.133
I'll never forget him.
Always be glad for that.

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:23.130
(soft piano music)

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:24.830
- Hey, everyone Thoraya here.

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:27.840
I wanted to say thank you
for watching this episode.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:29.950
I am still selling these
sweaters on my website.

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:32.060
If you're interested, I'll
link it in my description.

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:33.090
I love you guys.

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:35.163
And I'll see you next week.

00:13:35.163 --> 00:13:38.000
